          "id": "reconciliations",
          "title": "Bank reconciliations, supplier reconciliations and control accounts",
          "text": "## Reconcile rather than force a total\nA reconciliation explains why two independently maintained records differ. First establish that they concern the same account, date and currency. Some differences are book errors requiring journals; others are legitimate timing items requiring no duplicate posting.\n## Correct the cash book first\nBank charges, interest, standing orders, direct credits and dishonoured receipts appearing on the bank statement but absent from the books normally require cash-book updates after checking evidence. A bank error is not corrected by entering a fictitious business expense. Unpresented cheques and outstanding lodgements are commonly timing differences.\n## Complete worked bank reconciliation\nThe cash book shows £6,000 debit. The statement includes a £40 fee and a £700 customer direct credit not yet recorded. Corrected cash book = 6,000−40+700 = £6,660 debit. There are £900 lodgements not yet credited by the bank and £500 issued cheques not presented. Starting from corrected cash book, statement balance = 6,660−900+500 = £6,260 credit from the bank's viewpoint.\nWhy the signs? The cash book already includes the lodgement, while the statement does not, so deduct it to reach the statement. The cash book already deducted the cheques, but the statement has not, so add them. Starting from the statement reverses these adjustments. Label overdrafts explicitly rather than silently switch sign conventions.\n## Supplier statement reconciliation\nCompare invoices, credit notes, payments and dates line by line. If the supplier statement is £3,200 and the business ledger £2,800 because a valid £400 invoice was omitted from the books, record it after verification. If a £400 payment is recorded by the business but not yet received by the supplier, it can be a timing item instead. The identical numerical difference does not prove the same cause.\n## Control accounts\nA receivables control account summarises total customer activity; individual ledgers show each customer's balance. Reconcile both and investigate one-sided postings, duplicated invoices, incorrect allocations and balances in the wrong list. Agreement is a useful control but does not prove that all transactions are valid.\n## Practice\nA £120 cheque is correctly recorded in the cash book but unpresented at the bank. Should you credit bank again when preparing the reconciliation?\n### Answer\nNo. The business entry already exists. Record it as a timing difference; posting again would understate the cash book by £120. Investigate old outstanding items because a timing explanation should not be used indefinitely to conceal an error."
