          "id": "groups",
          "title": "Companies, group accounts, associates and equity",
          "text": "## A company is separate from its investors\nShare capital and share premium represent investment under their legal and accounting definitions. A loan creates a liability, not ownership. A dividend is a distribution, not a trading expense. Retained earnings accumulate profits and other relevant transfers less distributions; they are not a separate bank account containing cash.\nIf 10,000 £1 shares are issued at £3, proceeds are £30,000, nominal share capital £10,000 and share premium £20,000 under the simple assumptions. A business can have retained profits while holding little cash because resources are in inventory, receivables or equipment.\n## Why consolidate?\nA parent controlling a subsidiary presents the group as one economic entity. Control concerns power, exposure to variable returns and ability to use power to affect returns. Percentage ownership is evidence, not the sole definition. Consolidate subsidiary assets and liabilities line by line, eliminate the parent's investment and identify non-controlling interests.\n## Goodwill worked example\nA parent pays £120,000 for 80% of a subsidiary whose acquisition-date identifiable net assets at fair value are £100,000. With proportionate NCI of £20,000, goodwill = 120,000+20,000−100,000 = £40,000, before any impairment or other required adjustments. Pre-acquisition reserves form part of what was acquired; only relevant post-acquisition profit contributes to group reserves.\nA subsidiary's £30,000 post-acquisition profit is attributable £24,000 to the parent and £6,000 to NCI under this uncomplicated 80:20 case. Adjust fair-value depreciation and other consolidation items before allocating if the question requires it.\n## Eliminate internal results\nA £5,000 intra-group receivable/payable disappears because the group cannot owe itself. An internal sale is not external group revenue. If goods sold internally for £10,000 at 20% margin are half unsold to outsiders, eliminate £1,000 unrealised profit. Identify the seller because this can affect attribution to NCI.\n## Associates\nSignificant influence without control leads to different treatment. In a simple equity-method calculation, cost £60,000 plus 30% of £20,000 post-acquisition profit less 30% of £5,000 dividends gives £64,500 investment carrying amount before impairment and other adjustments. Do not add an associate's assets line by line as though it were controlled.\n## Practice\nWhy is a subsidiary's dividend to its parent not additional group revenue from outsiders?\n### Answer\nIt transfers resources within the group. Underlying subsidiary results are already included in consolidation; counting the internal dividend again would duplicate group income. Apply the required elimination and distinguish dividends paid to NCI, which leave the group."
