          "title": "Cash-flow statements, ratios and interpreting financial statements",
          "text": "## Profit is not cash\nCredit sales, inventory movements, accruals and non-cash charges make profit differ from operating cash. Buying equipment and raising debt affect cash without being the same as operating expenses or income. Classify cash flows as operating, investing and financing according to the exam's reporting requirements.\nAn indirect operating reconciliation starts with the specified profit measure, adjusts non-cash and non-operating items and incorporates working-capital changes. An increase in receivables ties up cash, so subtract it; an increase in operating payables postpones cash payment, so add it. Separate balances acquired with a subsidiary from organic operating movements.\n## Worked reconciliation\nProfit before relevant further adjustments is £20,000, depreciation £5,000 and a disposal gain £1,000. Receivables rise £4,000, inventory rises £2,000 and operating payables rise £3,000. Operating cash before other required items is 20,000+5,000−1,000−4,000−2,000+3,000 = £21,000. Disposal proceeds are separately considered as investing cash; the £1,000 gain is not the proceeds.\n## Ratios that answer different questions\n- Gross margin = gross profit/revenue; markup instead divides by cost.\n- Current ratio = current assets/current liabilities; quick ratio excludes inventory under the definition used.\n- Receivables days = average trade receivables/credit sales × days; payables and inventory use appropriate cost bases.\n- ROCE links relevant operating return to capital employed; gearing and interest cover assess aspects of financial risk.\nState definitions, use compatible periods and avoid mixing closing balances with averages without explaining the approximation.\n## Interpret, don't just calculate\nRevenue £200,000 and gross profit £60,000 gives 30% margin. Average receivables £20,000 gives 36.5 collection days on £200,000 annual credit sales. If collections slow, investigate credit terms, disputes, sales mix and recoverability. Do not automatically assert poor management. Seasonality, accounting policies, asset ages and acquisitions affect comparison.\nThe events-after-reporting-period distinction matters too: later evidence of an existing year-end condition may require adjustment, while a material new event may require disclosure. Notes on policies, estimates and related parties support interpretation; disclosure cannot cure an intentionally incorrect amount.\n## Practice\nInventory days are 50, receivables days 40 and payables days 30. What is the cash cycle, and is reducing it always beneficial?\n### Answer\n60 days. Reducing inventory may release cash but cause stockouts; extending supplier payment can lose discounts or disrupt supply. Assess the whole commercial effect rather than target the lowest number at any cost."
