          "id": "environment",
          "title": "Organisations, stakeholders and the economic environment",
          "text": "## Understand the organisation before the model\nA sole trader, company, public body and charity differ in ownership, legal identity, funding and accountability. A company owns its assets separately from shareholders; a not-for-profit can earn a surplus but directs resources toward its purpose. Define success using the organisation's objectives rather than assume profit is the only measure.\nStakeholders include owners, employees, customers, lenders, suppliers, regulators and communities. Identify their interests and influence. A power–interest matrix helps prioritise engagement but is not permanent: a quiet regulator can become highly interested after a breach. Explain the particular conflict and response, not only the stakeholder's name.\n## PEST and competition applied\nPolitical/legal changes affect permissions and obligations; economic changes affect demand, funding and costs; social/demographic changes affect customers and workforces; technology affects methods and access; environmental changes affect resources, physical exposure and transition costs. Translate each factor into a consequence for this business.\nPorter's five forces considers rivalry, entry, substitutes, supplier power and buyer power. A substitute meets the same need differently. Low switching costs and many suppliers can strengthen buyers; scale, intellectual property and access to distribution can affect entry. Industry attractiveness and one firm's internal capability are different questions.\n## Economics with numbers\nDemand movements caused by the product's price differ from shifts caused by income or preferences. If price rises 5% and quantity falls 10%, price elasticity is approximately −2, indicating elastic demand. Revenue becomes 1.05×0.90 = 94.5% of its old level: down 5.5%. A higher price did not produce higher revenue.\nFiscal policy concerns government spending and taxation; monetary policy influences rates and monetary conditions. Higher borrowing rates can reduce investment and consumer spending, but effects differ by sector and balance sheet. Nominal growth 7% with inflation 5% is exact real growth 1.07/1.05−1 = 1.90%, not a 7% rise in real activity.\n## Practice case\nA city café plans a late-night delivery service. Identify one economic, one stakeholder and one competitive issue and explain action.\n### Answer\nHigher loan rates can raise funding cost: assess the cash forecast and financing. Residents may oppose late-night traffic: consult and design delivery arrangements. Delivery platforms or home preparation are relevant competitive alternatives: compare differentiation and total platform fees before setting prices. The useful answer connects the fact, its consequence and a feasible response rather than writes three labels."
