          "id": "controls",
          "title": "Understanding systems, tests of controls and reporting weaknesses",
          "text": "## Control objectives and limitations\nInternal control seeks reasonable assurance over reporting, operations and compliance. Authorisation, segregation, reconciliations, physical controls, access controls and supervision address different risks. Collusion, human error and management override prevent absolute assurance.\nSeparate authorisation, custody, recording and reconciliation where practical. A small business may need compensating owner review and restricted access when full segregation is impossible. A signature proves little if no one checked the underlying evidence or followed up exceptions.\n## Understand before testing\nRecord processes through narratives, flowcharts or questionnaires and perform a walkthrough. Trace a purchase from order to receipt, invoice, payable and payment. This helps establish whether the described process is implemented. One walkthrough does not prove a monthly control operated effectively throughout the whole year.\nA test of controls examines control operation. A substantive test directly examines amounts or disclosures. Inspecting evidence of independent bank-reconciliation review is a control test; independently checking the bank balance and reconciling items can be substantive work. State the purpose clearly.\n## Worked control test\nControl: a finance manager reviews every monthly bank reconciliation and investigates old items. Select reconciliations across the period, inspect timely review evidence, examine follow-up of exceptions and reperform relevant aspects where appropriate. Enquiry alone is weak evidence. Repeated deviations may reduce reliance and require changed substantive procedures.\n## Communicate a deficiency\nUse deficiency, consequence and recommendation. If one employee changes supplier bank details and authorises payments, the risk is diversion and concealment. Recommend independent verification of bank changes through an established contact, restricted system rights and separate payment approval. “Improve segregation” is less useful because it omits implementation.\nDistinguish an ineffective control from a proven misstatement. Communicate significant matters promptly to the appropriate governance recipients; a management letter is not a substitute for a required opinion modification.\n## Practice\nA business has an impressive written purchase policy but routinely pays invoices without matching receipts. Is the policy enough for reliance?\n### Answer\nNo. Assess actual implementation and operation. Test the matching control if reliance is planned and respond to deviations. A document describing a control is not evidence it operated. Consider risk of nonexistent or incorrect purchases and design relevant further work."
